the Indians showed great sadness at its departure and allowed their voices to die away. And as it completely disappears, they made this act of reverence, worshipping it in the deepest humility. And then they all went back to their homes and the effigges and relics were returned to their shrines and temples.
印第安人对它的离去表现出极大的悲伤，并让他们的声音逐渐消失。当它完全消失时，他们进行了这种崇敬的行为，以最深沉的谦卑崇拜它。然后他们都回到了自己的家中，雕像和遗物被送回了他们的神龛和神庙。

15
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:39.000
So if you're part of that ceremony, which clearly Christabel de Molina, you know, the way he writes about it, It's respectful, isn't it? Yeah, he's respectful and he finds it clearly quite moving. There's this illusion of harmony and unity, that the world is back on its axis, that everything has been restored after the chaos of the Civil War. But there are three fracture lines that are widening all the time.
所以如果你是那个仪式的一部分，显然克里斯托瓦尔·德·莫利纳，你知道，他描写它的方式，它是尊重的，不是吗？是的，他很尊重，而且他显然觉得这相当感人。这是一种和谐与统一的错觉，世界回到了它的轴心上，内战后的混乱之后一切都已恢复。但有三条裂痕一直在扩大。

16
00:08:39.000 --> 00:09:26.000
So the first is between the Spanish and the Inca's. The second is among the Inca's themselves, and the third is among the Spaniards themselves. And that's arguably the most dangerous of the three. So if we start with the Spanish and the Inca's, when we ended last time, we were talking about how the Spanish were starting to behave very badly. They're starting to loot gold and silver. They're treating the priests with contempt. And, you know, a really big thing, Pizarro is starting to distribute estates, landed estates, to his fellow conquistadors. These are the encomiendas that become such a feature of kind of the settler colonial world of Spanish Peru. And he's got a big problem, hasn't he, that as more and more people come, so from Spain, so they want encomiendas as well.
第一条是在西班牙人和印加人之间。第二条是在印加人自己之间，第三条是在西班牙人自己之间。这可以说是三者中最危险的。所以如果我们从西班牙人和印加人开始，当我们上次结束时，我们谈到了西班牙人是如何开始表现得非常糟糕的。他们开始掠夺金银。他们轻视祭司。而且，你知道，一件非常大的事情，皮萨罗开始向他的征服者同伴分配地产，土地地产。这些就是后来成为西班牙秘鲁定居者殖民世界特征的“监护征赋制”。他有一个大问题，不是吗？随着越来越多的人从西班牙来到这里，他们也想要监护征赋。

17
00:09:26.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And that is an increasing source of pressure on his ability to maintain peace with the Inca's. Dead right. It's not just they want encomienders. It's that they arrive and they think they're going to get loads of gold. And they arrive and there's no gold because it's been given out. And so they say, well, if I can't have any gold, you better give me some land and somebody to work on the land. And so Pizarro is basically giving away more and more and ignoring requests from the court in Spain from royal officials. Please, when you stop distributing land that you don't own. And his other wheeze is to send them off into the jungle or into kind of remote deserts to go looking for lost cities with gold. A big feature. A big feature that will carry on.
这对他维持与印加人和平的能力构成了越来越大的压力。完全正确。他们想要的不仅仅是监护征赋。而是他们到达后认为自己会得到大量的黄金。但他们到达时却没有黄金，因为黄金已经被分光了。所以他们说，好吧，如果我不能得到黄金，你最好给我一些土地和一些在土地上干活的人。所以皮萨罗基本上是在分发越来越多的土地，并无视西班牙宫廷和皇家官员的要求。请停止分发不属于你的土地。他的另一个诡计是把他们打发到丛林或偏远的沙漠中去寻找失落的黄金之城。这是一个大特征。一个将持续下去的大特征。

18
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:37.000
So you start to get reports that from local towns and villages that people no longer see the Spaniards, the liberators who've entered the chaos of the Civil War. they see them as occupiers in their own right. And as so often with occupations, military occupations, a really key fault line is how the occupiers treat the local women. So as in Mexico, we had Pedro de Alvarado on the show last week, didn't we, an old associate of the rest of history. And you mentioned that he had got this wife, I think she was called Donna Louisa, wasn't she?
所以你开始收到来自当地城镇和村庄的报告，人们不再把西班牙人看作是进入内战混乱的解放者。他们把他们看作是名副其实的占领者。正如军事占领中经常出现的那样，一个非常关键的断层线是占领者如何对待当地妇女。所以就像在墨西哥一样，我们上周在节目中请到了佩德罗·德·阿尔瓦拉多，不是吗？他是历史其余部分的老搭档。你提到他有这个妻子，我想她叫唐娜·路易莎，对吧？

19
00:10:37.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Yeah. In Mexico, who is buried with him in the cathedral in Guatemala. And he became the governor of Guatemala. This is a very standard thing, not becoming the governor, but for the Spanish to take local wives, as they call them actually kind of mistresses or concumines. So Atta Welper had already given Pizarro a half-sister of his called Kisbe Kusi, who was 18 years old. The Spanish called her Inyes. Pizaro nicknamed her Pizpita, which is a water thrush from his native extramajura. It's a sweet name.
是的。在墨西哥，她和他在危地马拉的大教堂里合葬。他成为了危地马拉的总督。这是一件非常标准的事情，不是成为总督，而是西班牙人娶当地妻子，他们实际上称之为情妇或妾室。所以阿塔瓦尔帕已经给了皮萨罗一个他同父异母的姐妹，名叫基斯贝·库西，她当时18岁。西班牙人叫她伊内斯。皮萨罗给她起了个绰号叫皮兹皮塔，这是他家乡埃斯特雷马杜拉的一种水鸫。这是一个甜蜜的名字。

20
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:42.000
And he actually treated her pretty well. He called her his wife. She would dine with him and his captains. She had two children by him. Eventually he got bored of her and he married her off to another conquistador called Francisco de Ampero. And actually their descendants, the Amperos, ended up being one of Peru's most powerful political families. Because in Incan society, women do play kind of a key role as power brokers, as people who influence the policy of their husbands and their families. and, you know, with Alvarado or with Pizarro or whoever,
他实际上对她相当不错。他称她为他的妻子。她会和他以及他的船长们一起用餐。她为他生了两个孩子。最终他厌倦了她，把她嫁给了另一个名叫弗朗西斯科·德·安佩罗的征服者。事实上，他们的后代，安佩罗家族，最终成为了秘鲁最有权势的政治家族之一。因为在印加社会，女性确实扮演着关键的权力掮客角色，作为影响丈夫和家庭政策的人。而且，你知道，无论是阿尔瓦拉多还是皮萨罗，或者其他任何人，

21
